            "level": {
                "value": 1
            },
            "privateNotes": "",
            "publicNotes": "<p>Monaciellos delight in moral and institutional decay. They tempt with the easy course, encouraging laziness and apathy. They're the little voice that whispers that no one will notice, that it's not really worth the effort, that there's a quicker, easier solution if you just try just a little less hard.</p>\n<p>Monaciellos resemble small, sharp-toothed humanoids clad in the formal clothing of whatever institution or organization they're harassing. Most dress like religious figures—there's nothing a monaciello loves quite so much as a church lost to decadence and self-indulgence—but a monaciello might also pose as a well-to-do farmer, a civil servant, or even a soldier. There's always something slovenly and unkempt about their uniform of choice, and they keep a filthy magic bag thrown over one shoulder or near at hand. Monaciellos cause endless trouble by stealing small items, secreting them in their sack, and then bringing them out at the perfect moment to tempt mortals into indolence and sloth. These items are rarely impressive, but the gremlins always seem to have a drink or gold coin at hand when a mortal craves it most.</p><hr /><p>Like many fey, gremlins personify a natural force of the world: entropy. It's a fundamental truth that everything breaks, that every complex system, from a machine to a society, will inevitably grind itself down to nothing. Gremlins are happy to give those complex systems a friendly shove down the stairs.</p>\n<p>Nearly all gremlins live in the Universe, where they have fully acclimated to the plane. No one is quite certain why, but it seems likely that some long-forgotten gremlin managed to so irritate the Eldest to such a degree that their entire species was exiled—though it appears the Eldest missed a few, as some gremlins still lurk in the First World.</p>\n<p>Gremlins are usually found in an odd, parasitic relationship with mortal communities in the Universe. They don't need to eat or sleep, don't age or sicken, and never really die—but they do need to cause trouble. It's as essential to them as food and drink are to mortals, and nowhere do they get as many chances for chaos and confusion as in a mortal settlement. Gremlins thus become pests, living on the margins of society and bedeviling anyone and anything they can get their hands on.</p>",
            "publication": {
                "license": "ORC",
                "remaster": true,
                "title": "Pathfinder Feybound"
            }
        },
